<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uriah Butler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tubal Uriah Butler</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tubal Uriah 'Buzz' Butler was a Grenadian-born labour leader and preacher in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>. A former oilfield worker, he led the oilfield strike of June 1937, which spread into unrest across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad-and-tobago">Trinidad and Tobago</a> and led to the recognition of trade unions and labour reforms. Interned during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, he later led his own political party. He is a National Hero of Trinidad and Tobago.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uriah-butler.jpg" alt="Uriah Butler" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grueslayer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TnT_Fyzabad_Uriah_Butler_Statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Uriah Butler was born on 21 January 1897 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-georges-grenada">St. George's, Grenada</a>, and went to the Anglican School. Unable to find work after primary school, he volunteered at 17 for the British West Indies Regiment and served in the British Army from 1914 to 1918, stationed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a> he set up the Grenada Representative Government Movement and the Grenada Union of Returned Soldiers. In 1921, aged 24, he moved to south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and worked as a pipe-fitter at the Roodal Oilfields; according to Julius Garvey, he was influenced by the philosophy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey">Marcus Garvey</a>. He was also a Spiritual Baptist preacher.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Butler first came to notice in 1935, when he led a hunger march from the oilfields to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-of-spain">Port of Spain</a>. Expelled from the Trinidad Labour Party in 1936 for what it called extremist tendencies, he founded the British Empire Citizens' and Workers' Home Rule Party. When the strike over wages, working conditions, racism and exploitation began on 19 June 1937, police tried to arrest him at a meeting in Fyzabad; his supporters stopped them and a police officer, Charlie King, was killed. As the unrest spread from the oilfields to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> factories, a warrant was issued and Butler went into hiding, keeping in touch with the authorities through Adrian Cola Rienzi.</p><p>Promised safe passage to give evidence to the commission of enquiry, he was arrested when he came forward and was imprisoned from 9 September 1937 to May 1939, then detained again for the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>. Afterwards he led the Butler Home Rule Party, later the Butler Party, which won the largest block of seats in the Legislative Council; the Governor nonetheless left him out, and Albert Gomes became the first chief minister. In the 1956 general election the party won only two seats, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-williams">Eric Williams</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-national-movement">People's National Movement</a> regarded Butler as too unstable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Butler is regarded as the founding father of the Oilfields Workers' Trade Union and of the labour movement. He received the Trinity Cross, the country's highest honour, in 1970 and died on 20 February 1977. A statue honours him in Fyzabad, and the Uriah Butler Highway is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tubal Uriah Butler” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Arthur Cipriani led a reform movement in the 1920s, and the 1937 oilfield strikes and riots, inspired by <b>Uriah Butler</b>, led to unions and reform.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-trinidad-and-tobago">Colony of Trinidad and Tobago</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-georges-grenada">St. George's</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.05,-61.75&amp;year=1949" aria-label="St. George's on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Uriah Butler"><image href="/maps/globes/g30000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4853 3977h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4853 3977h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=12.05,-61.75&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
